findings.body:
This replaces the allowlist with reflecting whatever `Origin` the client sends, while still setting `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`.

**Failure mode:** any attacker-controlled site can make a credentialed cross-origin request to this API and read the response body (session cookies / credentialed fetch). The previous `ALLOWED` set blocked that; this opens it to every origin.

Native mobile apps do not use CORS. If mobile **webviews** need access, add their concrete origins to the allowlist instead of reflecting arbitrary ones.

Also, `origin ?? "*"` with credentials is invalid CORS when `origin` is null — browsers reject `*` + credentials — so that branch does not help clients and stays misconfigured.

```suggestion
const ALLOWED = new Set([
  "https://app.example.com",
  // add known mobile webview origins here
]);

export function corsHeaders(origin: string | null): Record<string, string> {
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return { Vary: "Origin" };
}
```
